Title: How Transformer Models Use Attention to Process Sequences

Overview
Transformers process sequences primarily through attention mechanisms that compute pairwise interactions among sequence elements, allowing the model to integrate information from any position in the sequence in parallel. Attention replaces recurrence and convolution as the core sequence-processing operation, enabling efficient learning of long-range dependencies and flexible context aggregation.

Core concept: attention as weighted aggregation
Attention views each element in a sequence as producing three vectors: a query (q), a set of keys (k), and values (v). To update the representation at a given position, the model compares that position’s query to the keys of all positions (including itself) to produce a set of attention weights. These weights (normalized by softmax) are used to form a weighted sum of the corresponding values. The result is a context-aware representation for that position.

Scaled dot-product attention (mathematical formulation)
Given matrices Q (queries), K (keys), and V (values) of shapes (n × d_k), (n × d_k), and (n × d_v), scaled dot-product attention computes:

    Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax( (Q K^T) / sqrt(d_k) ) V

- QK^T produces an n × n matrix of raw compatibility scores between all pairs of positions.
- Dividing by sqrt(d_k) stabilizes gradients when d_k is large.
- softmax is applied row-wise to convert scores for each query into a distribution over keys.
- Multiplying by V returns a weighted sum of value vectors for each query.

Intuition: each output position is a convex combination of all input values, with weights set by similarity between query and key vectors.

Multi-head attention
Instead of a single attention function, transformers use multiple parallel attention “heads.” For head i:

    Q_i = Q W_i^Q,  K_i = K W_i^K,  V_i = V W_i^V

where W_i^Q, W_i^K, W_i^V are learned projection matrices that map embeddings into different subspaces (dimension d_k for queries/keys and d_v for values). Each head computes attention independently:

    head_i = Attention(Q_i, K_i, V_i)

The heads are concatenated and linearly projected:

    MultiHead(Q,K,V) = Concat(head_1,...,head_h) W^O

Reasons for multi-head:
- Different heads can specialize to capture distinct types of relationships (syntactic, semantic, positional).
- Lower-dimensional projections allow more efficient attention while preserving expressive capacity when combined.

Transformer block architecture (how attention fits in)
A single transformer layer (encoder or decoder) typically contains:
1. Multi-head self-attention sublayer
2. Add & LayerNorm (residual connection)
3. Position-wise feed-forward network (FFN): two linear layers with an activation (usually ReLU or GELU)
4. Add & LayerNorm

Sequence of operations (encoder layer):
- X' = LayerNorm(X + MultiHead(X, X, X))
- Y = LayerNorm(X' + FFN(X'))

Residual connections help gradient flow and enable stacking many layers. Layer normalization stabilizes training.

Self-attention vs. cross-attention
- Self-attention: Q, K, V all come from the same sequence (used in encoder layers and decoder self-attention); each position attends to all positions in the same sequence.
- Cross-attention (encoder-decoder): Q comes from the decoder; K and V come from encoder outputs. This lets the decoder selectively incorporate encoder information.

Positional information
Attention on its own is permutation-invariant. Transformers add positional encodings to token embeddings so the model can use order:
- Absolute positional encodings: fixed (sinusoidal) or learned vectors added to token embeddings.
- Relative positional encodings: modify attention scores to directly encode relative distances, often improving generalization to longer sequences.

Causal masking for autoregressive decoding
During training and inference of autoregressive models (language generation), the decoder’s self-attention uses a causal (triangular) mask that blocks attention from position i to any future positions j > i. This ensures the model cannot peek at future tokens when predicting the next token. Formally, attention weights for positions j > i are set to −infinity before the softmax.

Computational characteristics
- Parallelism: Unlike RNNs, attention computes interactions for all positions simultaneously, enabling efficient parallel training on GPUs/TPUs.
- Complexity: Dense attention computes pairwise interactions, so compute and memory scale O(n^2 · d) for sequence length n and dimension d. This becomes a bottleneck for very long sequences.
- Trade-offs: High capacity to model global context at the cost of quadratic scaling. Many research efforts focus on reducing this cost (see variants below).

Small numeric example (3 tokens, d_k = 2) — illustrating one self-attention pass
Token embeddings e1=[1,0], e2=[0,1], e3=[1,1]. Use Q=K=V=[e1;e2;e3] and d_k=2 (sqrt(d_k)=1.414).
Compute QK^T:
[1 0 1
 0 1 1
 1 1 2]

Scale by 1.414 → approx:
[0.707 0 0.707
 0 0.707 0.707
 0.707 0.707 1.414]

Apply softmax row-wise, row 1 softmax of [0.707,0,0.707] ≈ [0.401,0.198,0.401].
Output for position 1 = 0.401*e1 + 0.198*e2 + 0.401*e3 = [0.802, 0.599].

This shows how attention mixes information from all positions based on similarity.

Advantages of attention-based processing
- Global receptive field: each output can consider the entire input.
- Efficient parallelization across sequence positions.
- Flexible pattern learning: attention weights can capture diverse pairwise relationships and are often interpretable.
- Strong empirical performance across NLP, vision, and other domains.

Limitations and common mitigations
- Quadratic scaling: mitigations include sparse attention patterns (local windows, strided, block sparse), low-rank approximations (Linformer), kernel-based linearized attention (Performer), locality-sensitive hashing approximations (Reformer), and memory-compressed or hierarchical models.
- Positional generalization: relative positional encodings and careful design can improve generalization to longer sequences.
- Interpretability caveats: attention weights are informative but not definitive explanations of model decisions—other mechanisms (residuals, FFN) also contribute.

Variants and extensions
- Sparse attention (Longformer, BigBird): mixes local and global attention to scale to long sequences.
- Linear attention (Performer, Linear Transformers): approximate softmax attention to achieve O(n) complexity.
- Reformer: uses locality-sensitive hashing to reduce attention cost.
- Encoder-only (BERT), decoder-only (GPT), encoder-decoder (original Transformer for translation) architectures adapt attention patterns to tasks.
- Relative positional encodings, rotary positional embeddings, and learned absolute embeddings are used in practice.

Practical implementation details
- Typical dimension sizes: d_model (e.g., 512–4096), number of heads h chosen so that d_k = d_model / h is reasonable (e.g., 64).
- Dropout applied to attention weights and FFN for regularization.
- Efficient batching and memory management are crucial for training large models.
- Mixed-precision (FP16/ bfloat16) and optimized matmul kernels are used widely.

Visualization and analysis
Attention matrices (softmax(QK^T / sqrt(d_k))) can be visualized per head and per layer to inspect which positions attend to which others. Patterns often reveal syntactic and semantic alignments (e.g., subject-verb dependencies, coreference).

Summary
Transformers use attention to compute context-aware representations by comparing queries to keys and producing weighted sums of values. Multi-head self-attention enables capturing multiple types of relationships in parallel. Combined with positional encodings, residual connections, and feed-forward networks, attention forms the core mechanism that allows transformers to process sequences efficiently and flexibly, with the trade-off of quadratic complexity that has motivated many scalable variants. The original "Attention Is All You Need" architecture established these principles and remains the foundation for subsequent transformer research and applications.
